entityDescription:
Intelligent Textiles Limited (ITL) is a UK based R&D company founded in 2002, that is a world-leader in electrically-active woven fabrics, or &ldquo;e-textiles&rdquo;. These e-textiles can perform a variety of electrical functions, including power and data transmission, heating, sensing and effecting, or translating many passive electrical components into the form of flexible, lightweight and robust fabrics. ITL maintains a significant portfolio of international patents.
ITL&rsquo;s commercial clients for wearable technology have included Nike, Armani, Microsoft and Mercedes Benz. ITL has specialised in dismounted soldier systems since 2009, contributing to a variety of successful programs for UK Ministry of Defence as both prime and subcontractor, including:
 &middot;RBDS (Reducing Burden on Dismounted Soldiers)- power transmission across e-textiles;
 &middot;MWPD (Man-Worn Power and Data)- data transmission across e-textiles;
 &middot;HAAP (Helmet as a Platform)- helmet-integrated video and nightvision;
 &middot;FSV2 (Future Soldier Vision 2)- power transmission across e-textiles;
 &middot;WTWE (Weaving the Winning Edge)- adaptive camouflage;
 &middot;FILS (Future Infantry Lethality System)- connecting digital weapon subsystems.

Internationally, ITL programs have included:
 &middot;SPIRIT for US Army and USMC (Soldier Power with Inductive Recharge and Intelligent Textiles)- integrated, modular power and data backplane for warfighter load-carriage;
 &middot;Broadsword&reg; for BAE Systems- integrated, modular power and data backplane for warfighter load-carriage;
 &middot;IdZ3.0 for Bundeswehr (Infanterist der Zukunft)- e-textiles for power and data networking and biometrics in uniforms and load-carriage.

In recent years, ITL has launched its own product, the Molliflex&reg; e-textile cable, which is currently under trial or contract for several NATO soldier system programs.
Key personnel at ITL include Asha Peta Thompson, with first degree and masters degree in Textiles, and Stan Swallow, with first degree in Industrial Design and doctorate in AI and Sensormatics.

